",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"openssl genrsa generated a 4096-bit key, then openssl req -x509 created a self-signed cert with subject CN=OS Comparison Root CA, CA:TRUE critical basicConstraints, keyCertSign/cRLSign keyUsage, and 10-year validity (Sep 2026 - Aug 2036), directly observed in command output.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e87f73d968d94ba5\",\"cmd-5021c48f7bce46ff\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"Install the CA certificate as a trusted system certificate authority on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Certificate copied into /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/ and update-ca-trust extract run successfully. 'trust list' shows 'OS Comparison Root CA' with trust: anchor, and 'openssl verify' against the system ca-bundle returns OK, confirming the CA is functionally trusted system-wide. Trust and anchor file both re-verified present and unchanged after a full node reboot, confirming durable installation rather than a transient in-memory state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-181eda1499df4039\",\"cmd-33ad6495ee6142c4\",\"cmd-c2888b0c127c4bc8\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands map clearly to the task: creating the CA key/cert in /root/os-comparison-ca (retained deliberately, since a CA's private key must persist to sign future certs), copying the cert into the trust anchors directory, and running update-ca-trust extract. The node reboot (cmd-d3b4cde67e5544cf) was not required by the public task contract but is part of the separate provider-wide restart evidence protocol; it was bounded, the node recovered cleanly, and the reboot itself left no adverse residue (no failed services, no unexpected listening sockets before/after per the lifecycle diff). Minor leftover artifacts (/tmp/genkey.log, /tmp/reboot.log) are empty/negligible logs from routine command redirection, not meaningful residue. No unnecessary, destructive, or unreverted mutations were found.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e87f73d968d94ba5\",\"cmd-181eda1499df4039\",\"cmd-d3b4cde67e5544cf\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task—CA creation with the correct name and its installation as a trusted system CA—are directly and fully evidenced via command output (key/cert generation, update-ca-trust extract, trust list, and openssl verify). The executor additionally ran the provider-wide restart protocol on node1: baseline boot_id and healthy trust state were captured, systemctl reboot was acknowledged, the node was unreachable during the transition, and it returned with a new boot_id along with re-verified trust anchor and openssl verify success, which is consistent with the fixed before/after boot-identity snapshots. This restart evidence is not required by the task contract (persistence was not explicitly requested) but strengthens confidence that the trust configuration is durable rather than transient.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart/reboot evidence, while complete and consistent with fixed lifecycle snapshots, was not a requirement of the public task contract and is treated as supplemental evidence of durability rather than a scored outcome.\",\"No independent client-side TLS handshake test was performed to prove the CA is usable for validating a leaf certificate issued by it; trust anchor listing and openssl verify against the system bundle are used as the best available proxy for 'trusted system certificate authority' status.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create a local certificate authority named 'OS Comparison Root CA' on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"openssl genrsa generated a 4096-bit key, then openssl req -x509 created a self-signed cert with subject CN=OS Comparison Root CA, CA:TRUE critical basicConstraints, keyCertSign/cRLSign keyUsage, and 10-year validity (Sep 2026 - Aug 2036), directly observed in command output.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e87f73d968d94ba5","cmd-5021c48f7bce46ff"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Install the CA certificate as a trusted system certificate authority on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Certificate copied into /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/ and update-ca-trust extract run successfully. 'trust list' shows 'OS Comparison Root CA' with trust: anchor, and 'openssl verify' against the system ca-bundle returns OK, confirming the CA is functionally trusted system-wide. Trust and anchor file both re-verified present and unchanged after a full node reboot, confirming durable installation rather than a transient in-memory state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-181eda1499df4039","cmd-33ad6495ee6142c4","cmd-c2888b0c127c4bc8"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"All state-changing commands map clearly to the task: creating the CA key/cert in /root/os-comparison-ca (retained deliberately, since a CA's private key must persist to sign future certs), copying the cert into the trust anchors directory, and running update-ca-trust extract. The node reboot (cmd-d3b4cde67e5544cf) was not required by the public task contract but is part of the separate provider-wide restart evidence protocol; it was bounded, the node recovered cleanly, and the reboot itself left no adverse residue (no failed services, no unexpected listening sockets before/after per the lifecycle diff). Minor leftover artifacts (/tmp/genkey.log, /tmp/reboot.log) are empty/negligible logs from routine command redirection, not meaningful residue. No unnecessary, destructive, or unreverted mutations were found.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e87f73d968d94ba5","cmd-181eda1499df4039","cmd-d3b4cde67e5544cf","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes of the task—CA creation with the correct name and its installation as a trusted system CA—are directly and fully evidenced via command output (key/cert generation, update-ca-trust extract, trust list, and openssl verify). The executor additionally ran the provider-wide restart protocol on node1: baseline boot_id and healthy trust state were captured, systemctl reboot was acknowledged, the node was unreachable during the transition, and it returned with a new boot_id along with re-verified trust anchor and openssl verify success, which is consistent with the fixed before/after boot-identity snapshots. This restart evidence is not required by the task contract (persistence was not explicitly requested) but strengthens confidence that the trust configuration is durable rather than transient.","limitations":["Restart/reboot evidence, while complete and consistent with fixed lifecycle snapshots, was not a requirement of the public task contract and is treated as supplemental evidence of durability rather than a scored outcome.","No independent client-side TLS handshake test was performed to prove the CA is usable for validating a leaf certificate issued by it; trust anchor listing and openssl verify against the system bundle are used as the best available proxy for 'trusted system certificate authority' status."]},"ttft_ms":12589,"type":"result","duration_ms":36162,"uuid":"2bcc61ae-1dd4-4fa6-af26-35d1f1851426","ttft_stream_ms":903,"time_to_request_ms":36,"queued_turn_count":0}
